WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.520
The sun was setting on the Norwegian port
of Bergen.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:31.560
It was early July, 1349, and off in the distance,
a lone ship was crossing the line of the horizon.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.540
The ship bobbed calmly, port to starboard,
coming closer into view.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.880
The inhabitants of Bergen noticed its tattered
sails and lack of movement on board.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.080
As gently as it had appeared, the ship entered
the harbour and run aground.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.540
Every sailor on board was dead.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.140
The people of Bergen were fully aware of the
events unfolding across the rest of Europe

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.280
over the previous two years: a mysterious,
malevolent, life-consuming force had spread

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.090
death and decay all over the continent.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.500
Until that day, Bergen had been spared.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.399
Norse mythology used to tell stories of Naglfar,
a ship made entirely of the nails and toenails

00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:11.560
of the dead.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.330
Naglfar would appear at the onset of Ragnarok,
the twilight of the Gods and the end of times.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.610
The ship of the dead would ferry the forces
of chaos to bring destruction upon the realms

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.410
of the Gods, and of humankind.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:29.320
Like Naglfar, the ghost ship of Bergen had
appeared from nowhere, carrying on board an

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.850
agent of dissolution and chaos, the scourge
of humanity.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.259
The Black Death.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:58.383
Revelation 6:8
“And I looked, and there was a pale green

00:01:58.383 --> 00:01:59.383
horse.

00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:00.383
Its rider was named Death, and Hades was following
after him.

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:01.383
They were given authority over a fourth of
the earth, to kill by the sword, by famine,

00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.383
by plague, and by the wild animals of the
earth.”

00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:03.383
[Note: I suggest the quote above is simply
shown on screen, not read aloud]

00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:04.383
How can we define the Black Death today?

00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:06.700
We could say it was the most successful human
conqueror in the history of mankind, if success

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.070
is measured by how many trees are cut down
to feed the funeral pyres.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:17.150
We could say it was the greatest strategist,
if greatness is measured by how little soil

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.950
remains to bury the dead.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.390
What we eventually defined as ‘the Black
Death’ was a mid-14th century pandemic of

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.100
bubonic plague that came knocking at the gates
of Asia, Europe, and Africa, rapping with

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.710
the soft paws of a sickly rat, a guest uninvited
with cruel intent.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:38.890
But if you look at the way the Black Death
spread, attacked, and preyed on humans; if

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:44.740
you consider the effectiveness with which
it encircled and annihilated entire Nations

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:49.019
… well, you’d be forgiven for thinking
that an even more sinister, unnatural power

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:53.480
was at play, hell-bent on bringing about the
end of times.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.670
But this wasn’t the first time that humanity
had withstood an invasion by this malevolent

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:04.580
angel: it had spread its wings back in the
6th Century, known then as the Justinian Plague.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:08.540
But too much time had passed; too many memories
had been lost.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.989
When the pandemic returned, humankind was
completely unprepared.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:18.160
The Black Death had laid dormant for eight
centuries, trapped in a purgatory of meagre

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.739
rodent prey.

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.550
Almost like a lifeless object, it awaited
its time of reprisal against its human enemy.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:30.550
We could not tell why it hated us, but we
did find out how much it could hate us.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:37.549
Black Death: Origins
It is still unclear where the Black Death

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:38.920
first came from.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.120
Possibly China, or maybe in the steppe region,
where a plague reservoir stretches from the

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.160
north-western shores of the Caspian Sea into
southern Russia.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.350
A plague reservoir looks like a vast area
of wilderness, almost entirely devoid of human

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:53.350
life.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.150
It may have been there, amongst a population
of marmots, rats, and other rodents, that

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.449
it first appeared, invisible to the naked
eye.

00:03:59.449 --> 00:04:05.660
The unaware mammals all carried the minions
of Black Death, a bacterium which later generations

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.560
would classify as Yersinia Pestis.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.390
This is the pathogen at the root of both the
bubonic and pneumonic plagues, and it was

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.620
believed to have been endemic to Central Asia.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.700
We don’t know the exact time in which the
disease started to spread from rodents to

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:27.220
humans, but we know of an early outbreak in
1331, which erupted in China during the rule

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.630
of the Yuan dynasty.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:32.850
This dynasty had been founded by Kublai Khan,
the grandson of Genghis Khan.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.850
The Mongols had been an irresistible force
for more than a century, but they had finally

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:38.060
met their match.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.560
Three years later, the Black Death had killed
more than 90 percent of the population of

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.310
Hebei province, near the Capital of Dadu,
or modern-day Beijing.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.410
The death toll surpassed 5 million people.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.590
These were the effects of the conquering disease
in just one province.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:05:00.690
As of 1200, China had a total population of
more than 120 million, but a census in 1393

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.260
found only 65 million Chinese accounted for.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.780
Almost half of the population of a great Empire
lay dead.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.540
As the Yuan Dynasty was replaced by the Mings,
many hundreds of thousand, even millions,

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.520
died in battle or starved.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.430
But many, many more millions had fallen prey
to the Black Death.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.870
After the first outbreaks, the rats and their
fleas, ridden with Yersinia Pestis, started

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.660
their journey westward, as stowaways to the
caravans travelling along the Silk Road.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.230
At the end of a long day’s travel, the merchants
would stop at the numerous caravansaries – or

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.620
resting places - dotting the Silk Road.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:40.500
These made perfect occasions for the possessed
fleas to infect rats, animals, and other travellers.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:45.290
Caravans upon caravans, criss-crossing the
plains of central Asia, unwittingly obeyed

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:49.980
the bidding of the Black Death, spreading
the plague across all of Asia and the Middle

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:50.980
East.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.690
By 1335 the Black Death had infected Persia.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:57.850
During that year, the local Mongol ruler Abu
Said died of bubonic plague.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:02.910
An estimated one third of all Persians died
in the ensuing epidemic, an event that marked

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.010
the beginning of the end of Mongol rule in
the region.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:10.280
More Central Asian cities and communities
fell one by one under the shadow of the Angel

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:16.130
of death: Talas, Samarkand, even Sarai- this
was the capital of another Mongol state, the

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.550
Khanate of the Golden Horde.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.570
It is as though the Black Death had a personal
grudge against the Mongols.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:25.260
Maybe it simply exploited their legendary
mobility to reach the four corners of the

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.860
World, exacting a toll after each passage.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.350
The plague did not discriminate amongst its
victims.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.210
Any King, Queen, or Khan could become easy
prey.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:43.490
Out of the Land of Darkness
After every sweep of the plague, each population

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.380
center would have lost at least 40 percent
of its citizens.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.700
Sometimes, the death tolls climbed as high
as 70 percent.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.650
Understandably, the inhabitants of stricken
cities fled in panic, but this only contributed

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.260
to further outbreaks of the disease.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.980
Soon, the Black Death had reached the Middle
East,, where some scholars had already taken

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:02.290
notice of the catastrophe that had nearly
obliterated Asia.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:06.490
Ibn al-Wardi, a Syrian writer, and later a
victim of the plague, recorded that the Black

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.390
Death “came out of The Land of Darkness.”

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:15.200
which may implicate Central Asia – or possibly
a much darker and more remote land beyond

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.200
this Earth.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:21.770
The Egyptian scholar Al-Mazriqi noted all
of Asia was depopulated, as far as the Korean

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:22.770
Peninsula.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.880
His own country was no luckier: Cairo, by
then the most populous city in Africa, lost

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.980
52% of its inhabitants.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.590
Almost 14,000 died in just two days.

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.990
The corpses were so many that in a rare occurrence,
religious authorities waived the required

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.919
prayers for the dead: there were simply too
many.

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:44.031
Ibn Battuta, the great traveler and chronicler
of the 14th Century, had noted in his Travel

00:07:44.031 --> 00:07:50.630
memoir that as of 1345, "the number that died
daily in Damascus had been two thousand"

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:54.970
Battuta also wrote that the survivors had
been able to defeat the plague through prayer.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.960
But Muslim religious practices may have played
in the Black Death’s favor.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.389
The Hajj, the annual pilgrimage to Mecca,
provided a great route for Yersinia Pestis

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:04.700
to seek new hosts.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.540
By 1349, the holy city of Mecca had been struck
by the plague.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:13.290
The Moroccan historian Khaldun left behind
an account of the Apocalyptic wasteland:

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:18.260
"Civilization both in the East and the West
was visited by a destructive plague which

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:23.220
devastated nations and caused populations
to vanish … Cities and buildings were laid

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:29.510
waste, roads and way signs were obliterated,
settlements and mansions became empty, dynasties

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.020
and tribes grew weak.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:34.320
The entire inhabited world changed."

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.570
Until recently, we (the descendants of the
survivors), assumed that the Sahara Desert

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:43.149
had stopped the outbreak, that the Black Death
had spared sub-Saharan Africa.

00:08:43.149 --> 00:08:47.670
Medieval written records for that region never
mention the plague, and the area lacks the

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:52.440
most macabre memento of the pandemic: the
‘plague pits’ or mass graves.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.750
It is not entirely clear how, but the thralls
of Black Death, the Yersinia, thanks to the

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:01.900
fleas and the rodents … somehow crossed
the inhospitable expanse of sand and infected

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.710
communities on both coasts of the continent.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:09.130
Archaeologists have found evidence of societal
collapse and abandonment of towns in sites

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:10.910
such as Akrokrowa, in Ghana or Ife, Nigeria.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:15.670
Other cities and settlements in East Africa
were also abandoned during the mid-14th Century,

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:19.750
from the coast of the Red Sea, down to Tanzania
and even Madagascar.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:24.890
Proof is scarce, as geneticists were not able
to secure DNA from African victims of that

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:30.460
period … and yet the patterns of sudden
collapse and de-population are too suspicious.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.270
The Black Death was definitely bent on total
World domination.

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.529
Asia had fallen.

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:38.300
The conquest of the Middle East and Africa
raged on.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.670
The next target was Europe.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:44.790
Waves of Yersinia Pestis could have infected
the Russian principalities at a very early

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.310
stage of the campaign for Asia.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.650
But the plans of the Black Death had been
momentarily thwarted by its early foes, and

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:53.160
victims: the Mongols.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.970
The Mongol state in Central Asia, the Khanate
of the Golden Horde, had converted to Islam,

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.130
and the trade with Christians was no longer
tolerated.

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:05.820
As a result, the caravan routes from Asia
to Europe had been cut off, meaning that contagion

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:10.740
of the plague stopped abruptly on the Mongol
border with the Russian principalities.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:15.450
But as I said, the Black Death was a cunning
strategist and would find its way to Europe,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:16.710
one way or another.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:46.560
Twenty-three Days
Before I move on to the conquest of Europe,

00:10:46.560 --> 00:11:51.130
allow me to take 
a look at the effects of the plague on an

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:52.130
individual.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:55.290
Yersinia Pestis initially likes to infect
rats and other rodents.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:59.940
It takes from ten to fourteen days for plague
bacteria to exterminate a contaminated rat

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:00.940
colony.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.370
For the following three days, the fleas infesting
these rats will lay in wait, fasting, until

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.090
new prey walks nearby.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:11.410
This prey can be another rat, but a hungry
flea will not think twice before attacking

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:12.490
a human.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:17.760
After an infected flea bites a human host,
Yersinia Pestis will invade his or her bloodstream.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.180
One of the first symptoms of contagion is
the swelling of a lymph node, most often in

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.740
the groin, thigh, armpit, or neck.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:29.089
The lymph node will grow in size, taking the
appearance of a swollen, blackened and painful

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:30.089
bubo.

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:32.170
Which gives its name to the bubonic plague.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:36.760
For three days, the infected individual may
feel fine, while the disease incubates.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:41.339
Then, early symptoms will appear, growing
in severity very quickly: fever, headache,

00:12:41.339 --> 00:12:43.300
chills, widespread weakness.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.800
After that, it’s only a matter of a three
more days.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:50.560
Then, in more than 80% of cases, the infected
individual will die.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.070
It takes 14 days for Yersinia to exterminate
a rat colony.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:57.330
Another three days before infected fleas contaminate
the first human.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.779
Six days later, the first human dies.

00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:05.400
That makes a 23-day period from the introduction
of plague amongst rats to the first human

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.590
death in a given community.

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:08.290
A strange number, 23.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.890
Try and divide 2 by 3 and what do you get?

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.070
Zero point 666.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:13.279
[Caption on screen: 0.666….]

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:14.800
And whose number is 666?

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:18.080
Of course, I am sure there is no relation,
I am just teasing …

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:23.399
But the speed and perversion with which the
Black Death killed, well, it did have a demonic

00:13:23.399 --> 00:13:24.399
quality.

00:13:24.399 --> 00:13:28.890
In some cases, plague bacteria became concentrated
in the lungs and caused symptoms similar to

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:29.890
pneumonia.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:34.000
This was pneumonic plague, and its course
was even more violent than the bubonic version.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.290
People ill with pneumonic plague could die
as early as after two hours of catching the

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:39.290
disease.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.500
What’s worse, pneumonic plague sufferers
can easily infect healthy individuals, as

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:45.459
they are beset with violent, bloody fits of
coughing.

00:13:45.459 --> 00:13:50.520
The droplets they expel can easily infiltrate
other people’s airways, resuming the cycle

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:51.520
of contagion.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:56.630
Surprisingly, however, the vast majority of
Black Death victims were not caused by human-on-human

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:57.780
contagion.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:01.710
Infections were almost exclusively due to
bites from fleas that had previously sucked

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:02.800
blood off a rat.

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:07.570
The tiny parasites could attack directly after
feasting on rat’s blood, or sometimes they

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.800
travelled unnoticed aboard another animal
or human host.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.670
The Black Death had to rely heavily on rats
for its invasion plans, and this had an effect

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:19.040
on both the geographic spread of the invasion
as well as its timeline.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:24.100
Rats thrived on ships, especially merchant
ships, which at that time were able to travel

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:27.209
40 kilometers, or 25 miles, every day.

00:14:27.209 --> 00:14:31.830
This is why the Black Death in Europe appeared
to conquer in leaps and bounds, rather than

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:35.280
following a linear path as it was in Asia.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.880
Rat fleas are parasites that are most active
in the warmer seasons, which explains why

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:44.100
the springs and summers of the mid-14th Century
became unusually lethal.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:48.960
‘Winter is coming’ … must have sounded
like a promise back then, as the plague lost

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.500
most of its contagion power in colder months.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:54.020
Doctors at that time could do little to fight
the Black Death.

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:58.450
The most common form of treatment was ‘lancing’,
or piercing, the buboes, which expelled a

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.020
reeking black liquid.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:03.640
Other methods involved leeching or bleeding
the ill, or washing their bodies with vinegar.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:09.660
A much later book, Edward IV's Plague Medicines
of 1480, reports a medicine commonly used:

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:14.070
"Take an egg that is newly laid, and make
a hole in either end, and blow out all that

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:15.070
is within.

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:19.800
And lay it to the fire and let it roast till
it may be ground to powder, but do not burn

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:20.800
it.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:24.580
Then take a quantity of good treacle, and
mix it with chives and good ale.

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:28.500
And then make the sick drink it for three
evenings and three mornings."

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:33.630
Certainly futile, but at least the ale was
good.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:41.740
Europe shall fall
In 1344, the Golden Horde decided to lay siege

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:45.190
to the port city of Kaffa, on the Crimean
peninsula.

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:50.029
Since the late 1200s, Kaffa had been under
the rule of Genoa, one of the Four Italian

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:51.029
Sea Republics.

00:15:51.029 --> 00:15:56.040
The Genoese resisted the Mongols for three
years, but in 1347, reinforcements from the

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.990
Golden Horde arrived, and they brought with
them something undesired: the plague.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:05.730
An Italian lawyer, Gabriele De Mussis, described
the last stages of the siege:

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:10.450
"The whole army was affected by a disease
which overran the Mongols and killed thousands

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.170
upon thousands every day.

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:17.279
The Mongol leader ordered corpses to be placed
in catapults and lobbed into the city in hopes

00:16:17.279 --> 00:16:20.550
that the intolerable stench would kill everyone
inside."

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.820
Catapulting your own dead soldiers inside
the city walls… that’s a tactic that would

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:27.320
certainly please General Black Death.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:32.990
But Gabriele’s is the only account of such
an event, so it may have been an exaggeration.

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:36.460
Nonetheless, the infection soon spread within
the city walls.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:40.399
And from there, pushed out by the double threat
of the Mongols and the plague, the Genoese

00:16:40.399 --> 00:16:42.399
merchants escaped by ship.

00:16:42.399 --> 00:16:47.350
The war waged by the Black Death in Asia had
been on land, relying on caravans and the

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.050
fast Mongol cavalry.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:52.470
The invasion of Europe would be a more nautical
one.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:57.890
In October of 1347 one of the Genoese ships
landed in the Sicilian port of Messina.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:01.959
It was clear to the port officials that something
was wrong: most of the crew were dead, and

00:17:01.959 --> 00:17:05.829
the rest spoke about a mysterious disease
that had ravaged them.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:09.500
The survivors were prevented from leaving
the ships and, after a few days they were

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:10.589
all dead.

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:13.949
A contemporary account reads:
“The sailors brought in their bones a disease

00:17:13.949 --> 00:17:19.600
so violent that whoever spoke a word to them
was infected and could in no way save himself

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:20.600
from death...

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.900
There then appeared, on a thigh or an arm,
a pustule like a lentil.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:28.669
From this the infection penetrated the body
and violent bloody vomiting began.

00:17:28.669 --> 00:17:33.799
It lasted for a period of three days and there
was no way of preventing its ending in death.”

00:17:33.799 --> 00:17:36.940
So, ‘whoever spoke a word’ to the sick
was infected.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:38.630
No one had thought about the rats.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:43.320
Loyal to their black Master, they had scurried
over the ropes connecting the ship to the

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:44.320
docks.

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:48.019
These tightrope walkers were the executioners
of Europe.

00:17:48.019 --> 00:17:51.940
The outbreak at Messina is often cited as
the first landing of the Black Death in Europe.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:57.179
But the Genoese sailors from Kaffa made several
other stops, both before and after Messina,

00:17:57.179 --> 00:18:00.609
every time spreading the plague in so-called
‘metastatic leaps.’

00:18:00.609 --> 00:18:05.700
Black Death had arrived in Constantinople
in May 1347, where it had launched from the

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.179
Adriatic coast.

00:18:07.179 --> 00:18:09.179
It reached Marseilles in September.

00:18:09.179 --> 00:18:13.580
Throughout Autumn, the Genoese, or those who
had been infected by them, landed in all major

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:18.139
trading hubs of the Mediterranean: Dubrovnik,
Venice, Pisa, Genoa, Valencia.

00:18:18.139 --> 00:18:23.289
All these ports were the bridgeheads for the
invasion force, which then broke through to

00:18:23.289 --> 00:18:24.350
the mainland.

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:28.690
More merchant ships fell to the Black Death,
resulting in more metastatic leaps.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:33.650
By March 1348, Southern France, Northern Spain
and almost all of Italy were living under

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.570
the shadow of the Conqueror.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:39.330
The wealthy city-state of Florence had been
struck harder than other Italian towns.

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:44.510
It is estimated that 70% of its citizens died
within the few early months of the plague.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:48.379
The outbreak inspired seminal author Boccaccio
to write his ‘Decameron’.

00:18:48.379 --> 00:18:52.899
This is how he describes the massacre:
“It first betrayed itself by the emergence

00:18:52.899 --> 00:18:58.220
of certain tumours in the groin or the armpits,
some of which grew as large as a common apple,

00:18:58.220 --> 00:18:59.399
others as an egg...

00:18:59.399 --> 00:19:01.889
Many died daily or nightly in the public streets.

00:19:01.889 --> 00:19:06.731
Of many others, who died at home, the departure
was hardly observed by their neighbors, until

00:19:06.731 --> 00:19:09.619
the stench of the bodies carried the news.”

00:19:09.619 --> 00:19:11.320
The Black Death then crept northward.

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:15.299
From the French Mediterranean bridgeheads,
the hordes of Yersinia-infected rats moved

00:19:15.299 --> 00:19:17.580
inland and reached the Atlantic ports.

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:21.630
It was a ship from Bordeaux that took the
plague to England: the first town to be infected

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:26.769
was Melcombe Regis, where the epidemic broke
out shortly before June 24th.

00:19:26.769 --> 00:19:28.900
London fell in August 1348.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:34.039
By January 1349, the population was so depleted,
especially of clergymen, that the Bishop of

00:19:34.039 --> 00:19:39.090
Bath wrote this to his diocese:
“The plague... has left many parish churches...

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.929
without parson or priest to care for their
parishioners...

00:19:41.929 --> 00:19:45.870
Therefore, if parishioners are on the point
of death and cannot secure the services of

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:50.730
a priest, then they should make confession
to each other... if no man is present, then

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:51.980
even to a woman.”

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:55.929
The whole Kingdom had been contaminated by
the end of 1349.

00:19:55.929 --> 00:19:59.749
Further North, Norway was the first Scandinavian
country to be invaded.

00:19:59.749 --> 00:20:02.769
Oslo fell first, in the autumn of 1348.

00:20:02.769 --> 00:20:07.179
The outbreak in Oslo was mercifully stopped
by the advent of winter weather, but it broke

00:20:07.179 --> 00:20:08.179
out again in the early spring.

00:20:08.179 --> 00:20:12.850
The following year, the Black Death completed
its pincer movement against Norway, with a

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:17.409
second landing: the Ghost Ship at Bergen,
plague’s own Naglfar, was a ship of wool

00:20:17.409 --> 00:20:20.960
merchants from the English port of King’s
Lynn.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:24.169
Taking Oslo was a strategic masterstroke for
the Black Death.

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:28.909
The city was a major trading hub for the Hanseatic
fleet, a League of rich and powerful merchant

00:20:28.909 --> 00:20:31.470
towns stretching from the North Sea to the
Baltic.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:36.120
Needless to say, their merchants sailing off
Oslo disseminated a cargo they could not fathom

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:41.320
to new destinations – Denmark, Sweden, and
Northern Germany soon fell.

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:45.159
The new front opened in the spring of 1350
and moved southward, crushing the populace

00:20:45.159 --> 00:20:48.940
with the same plague invasion that had entered
Southern Germany from Austria.

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:52.899
Now that Black Death had spread to almost
all of Europe, it plotted its next move.

00:20:52.899 --> 00:20:58.059
This was one that would spell disaster for
many conquerors in later centuries: invading

00:20:58.059 --> 00:20:59.059
Russia.

00:20:59.059 --> 00:21:02.519
It had already tried, entering from the East,
but the Golden Horde had stopped the caravans

00:21:02.519 --> 00:21:03.799
along the Silk Road.

00:21:03.799 --> 00:21:07.039
Now, nobody would stop the scourge of Nations.

00:21:07.039 --> 00:21:11.989
The plague entered the principality of Novgorod
in the late autumn of 1351, but the onset

00:21:11.989 --> 00:21:13.370
of the cold stopped the outbreak.

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:18.879
But is was only temporarily: the progress
of the disease resumed in the spring of 1352,

00:21:18.879 --> 00:21:20.299
reaching Moscow the following year.

00:21:20.299 --> 00:21:24.109
The Black Death reached the border with the
Golden Horde, this time from the west, bringing

00:21:24.109 --> 00:21:25.830
its Odyssey full circle.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.070
In the same year, the pandemic petered out.

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:32.360
Iceland and Finland were the only countries
that had evaded the Apocalypse; the rest of

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:38.739
the continent was on its knees.

00:21:38.739 --> 00:21:42.419
After the End
The death toll is still debated.

00:21:42.419 --> 00:21:47.780
According to conservative estimates, the Black
Death had swept away 20 to 30 percent of Europe’s

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:48.780
population.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:52.649
Recent calculations place the number much
higher, near 60 percent.

00:21:52.649 --> 00:21:58.239
That’s 50 million men, women, and children
who lost their lives because of the bite of

00:21:58.239 --> 00:22:02.450
the tiniest and humblest parasite one could
imagine.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:06.529
Humanity simply did not have the tools to
combat this invisible foe.

00:22:06.529 --> 00:22:11.049
Doctors did not have a cure, and even preventative
measures were insufficient.

00:22:11.049 --> 00:22:15.320
Germ theory was still centuries away, but
authorities did understand that the proximity

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:18.600
of contaminated individuals would spread the
disease.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:23.009
Several cities implemented quarantines, preventing
travelers from entering within their walls.

00:22:23.009 --> 00:22:28.220
What they could never realistically hope to
contain was rats and fleas, creeping in from

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:31.350
everywhere undetected.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.169
What did stop the Black Death, then?

00:22:33.169 --> 00:22:35.289
It was a combination of two elements.

00:22:35.289 --> 00:22:40.979
First, the onset of longer, colder winters
that killed off plague-carrying fleas.

00:22:40.979 --> 00:22:43.999
Second, the disease itself was too effective
to be efficient.

00:22:43.999 --> 00:22:47.269
The plague killed off its victims too quickly,
rats and humans alike.

00:22:47.269 --> 00:22:51.260
This prevented them from travelling further
afield, carrying the bacteria to more and

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.269
more hosts.

00:22:52.269 --> 00:22:57.510
The Europe that slowly recovered after the
year 1353 was a transformed continent.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:00.370
Surprisingly, some of the societal changes
were for the better.

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.559
The poorer sections of the population enjoyed
a better diet, better working conditions and

00:23:04.559 --> 00:23:06.159
a longer life expectancy.

00:23:06.159 --> 00:23:10.429
This was a consequence of the reduction in
workforce: it prompted feudal lords to increase

00:23:10.429 --> 00:23:14.620
their labourers’ wages and to find innovative
ways to increase the productivity of their

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:15.620
lands.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:19.010
The epidemic also had an impact on the spread
of culture and education.

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:23.289
As travel to traditional University cities
became dangerous, new scholarship centres

00:23:23.289 --> 00:23:27.519
were founded, allowing for the multiplication
and diversification of academia.

00:23:27.519 --> 00:23:33.529
The Black Death may have eventually died out,
but its carriers, the Yersinia Pestis bacteria,

00:23:33.529 --> 00:23:35.249
evolved into a different strain.

00:23:35.249 --> 00:23:40.639
A strain more selective , yet equally malicious:
one that would be able to pick its victims

00:23:40.639 --> 00:23:42.440
based on their age.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:48.200
This became the second wave in 1361, one that
would target specifically the younger rungs

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:53.299
of society, the most innocent and vulnerable:
this would become known as ‘The Second Pestilence’,

00:23:53.299 --> 00:24:01.440
or ‘The Plague 
of Children’.

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:26.830
But 
this is a story for another day.

